# --- registry -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by setup/lib/models.sh; passed to llama-server as environment and
# ignored there. MODEL_SWA=yes means the profile MUST carry --swa-full —
# without it every Claude Code turn re-processes the whole prompt.
MODEL_TITLE=Qwen3.6-35B-A3B · MTP-Q4, 21.7 GiB · PRODUCTION since 04.09.2026 17:44 · 1.9-2.9x flashnext on ALL TWELVE measured cells at 27.7 GiB of GTT against 91.0 — speed measured, QUALITY NOT: below flashnext on published benchmarks and this quant has no imatrix provenance, so the switch is an operator judgement being tested on real work
# MODEL_SWA=no — MEASURED, not assumed: config.json of Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B
# (fetched 04.09.2026) has no `sliding_window` key at all. What it has instead
# is `layer_types`, 40 entries alternating three `linear_attention` and one
# `full_attention` (`full_attention_interval: 4`). Linear attention is not a
# sliding window — it keeps a fixed-size recurrent state per sequence rather
# than a capped window of tokens — so --swa-full has nothing to do here.
MODEL_SWA=no
# MODEL_SOURCE — where this model comes from, so the profile can fetch it.
# `<repo> <pattern>…`: every pattern is a substring of a path in that repo and
# every matching .gguf is fetched. Verified 04.09.2026 against the repo's own
# tree listing, by matching the exact filename this profile's -m points at —
# not by guessing.
#
# The mmproj is NOT in this list yet, and the reason is a defect rather than a
# decision: this repo's model directory is one flat namespace, and unsloth
# names the vision encoder after its PRECISION, not after its model. So
# `mmproj-F16.gguf` here (899,283,680 B) and `mmproj-F16.gguf` for qwen38
# (927,607,488 B, on this disk since 17.08.) are the same path. Adding the
# pattern would have fetch-model.sh resume into another profile's file.
# Measured 04.09.2026; see the open points at the end of this file.
MODEL_SOURCE=havenoammo/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MTP-GGUF MTP-UD-Q4_K_XL
#
# NOT UNSLOTH, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS LINE. unsloth converts with
# --no-nextn, so NONE of their GGUFs for this model carry its MTP head — and
# the head is worth more than anything else measured here (see SPECULATION).
# havenoammo publishes the same model converted WITH it. Verified before the
# download and again after: `nextn_predict_layers 1`, `block_count 41` (the
# 40 layers plus the MTP one), four `blk.40.nextn.*` tensors, 753 in total.
#
# THE PRICE IS PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A REAL ONE. unsloth publishes an imatrix
# with their UD quants; this publisher documents none. So the file is trusted
# for its STRUCTURE, which was read off it, and not for its calibration, which
# nobody can check. On a model whose published quality already sits below the
# incumbent's, that is a second unknown stacked on the first. The clean way
# out is in the open points: this repo can convert the checkpoint itself, MTP
# head included — `conversion/qwen.py:636` registers exactly this
# architecture and its chain carries `supports_mtp_export = True`.
#
# WHY Q4 AND NOT Q8, having measured that Q8 costs only 5-8 %: the operator
# asked for maximum throughput. Both files are on disk and the swap is one
# line. The quant comparison that measured the 5-8 % stays below, because it
# is what makes this a CHOICE rather than an assumption.
#
# THE Q8 MEASUREMENT, kept because it is the surprising one.
# Every other profile here serves Q4_K_XL, because the received wisdom is that
# decode on this machine is bandwidth-bound and doubling the weights halves it.
# MEASURED 04.09.2026 on this model, one variable, same window, same flags,
# same build — bench/reports/2026-09-04_1048_speed_qwen36-q8-rocm against
# …_0937_speed_qwen36-ngram-pmin075:
#
#                    Q4      Q8     Q8/Q4
#     prose d512    35.2    33.4     0.95
#     prose d36k    29.5    27.5     0.93
#     count d36k   161.6   155.3     0.96
#     copy  d8192  281.5   258.9     0.92
#     prefill d512 683.3   635.5     0.93
#     prefill d36k 662.1   609.5     0.92
#
# **Q8 costs 5-8 %, not half.** The wisdom is not wrong in general; it does not
# reach this model. Only 3B of 35B parameters are active per token, so the
# weight traffic per token is small enough that it is not what decode waits
# for here — and with speculation accepting 100 % on count and copy, several
# tokens come out of one forward pass anyway.
#
# WHY THAT DECIDES THE PROFILE. The open question about this model is not
# speed, it is QUALITY: the published agentic-coding figures put it below the
# incumbent, and 3B active against 6B is why. Against that, spending 5-8 % so
# that the quantisation adds nothing further is the obvious trade. Q8_K_XL is
# the closest thing to the released checkpoint short of BF16 — which this
# machine must not run (an operator handover reports BF16 collapsing to ~25 %
# of Q8 on Strix Halo; not measured here and not worth measuring).
#
# Both files stay on disk, so this comparison can be re-run without a
# download, and so the fidelity/throughput trade can be reversed in one line.
# Memory, for scale: 40.83 GiB of GTT at Q8, 25.84 at Q4, 27.71 at the MTP-Q4
# this profile serves — against flashnext's 91.0 in every case.
# TEMPLATE_LEVELS — which reasoning levels this model's chat template renders.
# `no-levels` is the MEASURED answer, not the default: the template shipped
# inside the GGUF (read 04.09.2026 out of the Hugging Face API's `gguf`
# metadata, 8,057 chars) contains `enable_thinking` and `preserve_thinking`
# and nothing else — no `reasoning_effort`, no `thinking_budget`. So thinking
# is a switch here, not a dial, and a mode that named a level would be silently
# dropped by the template. setup/gateway/modes.py refuses one instead.
TEMPLATE_LEVELS=no-levels
# Every vocabulary word maps to the one thing the template understands. The
# same shape gemma26 and batch use, and for the same reason.
MODES=low:on  medium:on  high:on  xhigh:on  max:on
# MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN — what one token of context costs in KV, MEASURED by
# the two-point method: load at -c 65536 and at -c 262144 and take the GTT
# difference, so the weights and the compute buffers cancel out of the
# subtraction rather than having to be estimated.
MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN=21.0
MODEL_KV_SOURCE=04.09.2026, two-point under bench/sideserver.py on build-rocm-patched-b10702-11-gc799f1014: the server's own GTT (per DRM client) read 21.90 GiB at -c 65536 and 25.84 at -c 262144, i.e. 3.94 GiB over 196,608 tokens = 21.01 KiB per token, 5.25 GiB at the full window. The -c 262144 point is bench/reports/2026-09-04_0924_speed_qwen36-c262144; the -c 65536 point ran budget.py --observe and nothing else, so it produced NO report of its own and both raw outputs are kept verbatim in bench/reports/2026-09-04_0940_qwen36-vs-flashnext/memory-observations.txt. The estimate derived from the checkpoint's config beforehand was 20.0 — 5 % low, which is the one time on this machine such a derivation has come close (flashnext's was 56 % low).
# MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB — what GTT shows with the KV taken out, i.e. the weights
# the GPU really pins plus its compute buffers. Computed from EACH of the two
# points above independently, and they agree to the second decimal: 21.90 -
# 1.31 = 20.59, and 25.84 - 5.25 = 20.59. That agreement is the check, not a
# coincidence — it is what says the model is linear in the window and that
# neither point was taken in a disturbed state.
#
# NOTE it is BELOW the 20.82 GiB file size. So about 0.23 GiB of the file is
# never pinned. Not investigated; it is the harmless direction (the guard
# charges more than the model takes) and it is what the two-point method is
# for. Do not "correct" it upwards to the file size.
#
# THE FIGURE BELOW IS THE MTP-Q4's, AND IT IS ONE POINT, NOT TWO. The server's
# own GTT (per DRM client) read 27.71 GiB at -c 262144 in the exact shape this
# profile serves; minus 21.0 KiB/token x 262144 = 5.25 GiB of KV, that leaves
# 22.46. It rests on the KV RATE from the Q4 two-point above rather than on
# its own pair — the rate is quant-independent, the KV being f16 in every
# case. A second window at this shape would be the stronger evidence and
# nobody has loaded one.
#
# NOTE THE SIGN HAS FLIPPED. The unsloth files came out BELOW their file size
# (20.59 against 20.82); this one comes out 0.80 GiB ABOVE its 21.66. That is
# the draft context: the MTP path builds a second graph and it needs its own
# buffers. Do not read the two shortfalls as the same phenomenon.
#
# For the other two files, measured the same morning and kept for the swap:
#   unsloth UD-Q4_K_XL   base 20.59   (two-point, both points agreeing)
#   unsloth UD-Q8_K_XL   base 35.58   (one point, GTT 40.83)
MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB=22.46
# MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB — what stays resident OUTSIDE GTT. RssAnon of the
# RUNNING server, the one-line check this repo requires: 0.24 GiB at Q4/-c
# 65536, 0.25 at Q4/-c 262144, 0.27 at Q8/-c 262144, and 0.29 in the MTP shape
# this profile serves — four loads, and the figure barely moves with the
# window, the quant or the draft head.
# Re-measure if LLAMA_BIN moves: 28.6 GiB was found where 0.31 was declared on
# 31.08., which is how this rule got written.
#
# "THE LARGEST" WAS TRUE OF SIDE-SERVER LOADS AND IS NO LONGER TRUE AT ALL.
# This model went into PRODUCTION on 04.09.2026 at 17:44, and the running
# server reads RssAnon 0.38 GiB — above all four figures above. The four were
# taken behind bench/sideserver.py, which runs without the gateway and with a
# cold -cram; the production shape is more. 0.09 GiB is small enough that the
# guard is unaffected, and the sentence claiming 0.29 was the maximum has been
# removed rather than left to be believed. The declared value stays at 0.29
# until somebody takes a second production reading to declare instead of one.
MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB=0.29
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY THIS MODEL IS HERE
# ----------------------
# The operator asked for a FASTER model at sufficient quality — flashnext is
# the incumbent and it is large: 103.7 GiB of file, 80.8 GiB measured in GTT.
# This one is 35.8 GiB at the Q8 this profile settled on, and the interesting
# number was never the file size but the ACTIVE parameter count: 3B of 35B per
# token against flashnext's 6B. That is what decode time is made of here — and
# it is also, on the published figures, why this model is the weaker coder.
# One fact, both consequences.
#
# THE HYPOTHESIS HELD, AND THE QUANT MEASUREMENT IS WHY IT MATTERS. Decode was
# expected to be weight-bandwidth-bound, which would have made Q8 cost half
# the speed; measured, it costs 5-8 % (see MODEL_SOURCE). So the fast model
# can be run at near-checkpoint fidelity, and the remaining quality question
# is about the MODEL and not about how it is stored here.
#
# Not everything in the paragraph above was measured here — the file size is read
# off the disk, everything else comes from the model's own config.
#
# WHAT IT IS, from the checkpoint's own config.json (fetched 04.09.2026 from
# Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B; every figure below is a READ, not a measurement):
#
#     num_hidden_layers          40
#     layer_types                30 x linear_attention, 10 x full_attention
#     full_attention_interval    4        (every 4th layer keeps a real KV)
#     num_key_value_heads        2
#     head_dim                   256
#     num_experts                256, num_experts_per_tok 8
#     moe_intermediate_size      512, shared_expert_intermediate_size 512
#     max_position_embeddings    262144
#     mtp_num_hidden_layers      1        (see SPECULATION below)
#     vocab_size                 248320
#
# THE ARCHITECTURE TAG SAYS qwen35, AND THAT IS NOT A MISTAKE. The GGUF
# declares `general.architecture = qwen35moe` and the checkpoint declares
# `model_type: qwen3_5_moe` — Qwen3.6 reuses Qwen3.5's architecture and
# changes the weights and the post-training, not the graph. Consequence, and
# it is the good one: **no new llama.cpp build is needed.** Checked 04.09.2026
# against the built artifact rather than the source, because a tree and a
# binary are different things — `strings bin/libllama.so | grep '^qwen35moe$'`
# hits in build-rocm-patched (b10702-11-gc799f1014) and in the pinned
# cachelookup build alike.
#
# WHAT ONE TOKEN OF CONTEXT COSTS — DERIVED, NOT MEASURED. Written down so the
# measurement has something to disagree with, and marked so nobody mistakes it
# for the real thing:
#
#     10 full-attention layers x 2 KV heads x 256 head_dim
#       = 5,120 elements for K, the same for V
#     10,240 elements x 2 B (f16) = 20,480 B
#     -> 20.0 KiB per token, i.e. 5.0 GiB at -c 262144
#
# plus a per-SEQUENCE constant for the 30 linear layers (conv + SSM state,
# ~2 MB per layer at float32, ~60 MB total) which does not grow with the
# window. For scale: flashnext measured 37.3 KiB/token, qwen38 74.3.
# THE ESTIMATE MADE FOR FLASHNEXT BEFORE ITS DOWNLOAD WAS 56 % LOW. Treat
# this one the same way — it is arithmetic over a config file, and the config
# file does not know what llama.cpp allocates.
#
# --- the arguments, and what is and is not known about each -----------------
#
# -c 262144: the model's own max_position_embeddings, and CHECKED against the
#   measured KV figure rather than the estimate — 21.0 KiB/token x 262,144 =
#   5.25 GiB, which is what the observed GTT shows. The full window is
#   affordable here in a way it has never been on this machine: flashnext
#   pays 37.3 KiB/token for the same thing and had to be cut to 240,640.
# -np 1: NOT a fresh decision. Two independent defects were measured on this
#   hardware at two slots (setup/env/qwen38.env carries the matrix): the
#   gfx1151 HIP race and slot-restore poisoning. Neither is model-specific —
#   both are properties of this GPU and this server — so a new model does not
#   get to reopen the question by default.
# -ub 512 -b 2048: MEASURED ON THIS MODEL, 04.09.2026, and it is the one
#   place where the raw number and the right answer point opposite ways.
#   Three sideserver runs in the serving shape, reports 2026-09-04_1152
#   (the profile), _1233 (-ub 2048), _1236 (-b 4096 -ub 4096 — the shape a
#   Strix Halo handover the operator brought recommends).
#
#   ON THE RAW PREFILL RATE, THE BIGGER UBATCH WINS, by a lot:
#
#                      ub512/b2048   ub2048/b2048   ub4096/b4096
#     pp d8192 count      624.9          814.7          783.8
#     pp d8192 copy       526.3          747.8          763.1
#     pp d36k  copy       351.9          452.8          463.9
#
#   AND THE CACHE REUSE COLLAPSES UNDERNEATH IT, monotonically:
#
#     cached % d8192       94.7           77.9           55.5
#     cached % d36k        98.7           94.5           88.8
#
#   That is not noise. Prompt-cache reuse is QUANTIZED TO THE UBATCH — this
#   repo measured the same effect on qwen38 (98.7 % against 94.5 % on
#   identical bodies) and recorded it as a bonus of -ub 512. Here it is the
#   whole argument, because what an agent actually pays on a warm turn is
#   `uncached tokens / prefill rate`, and the two columns multiply:
#
#     seconds to re-prefill  ub512/b2048   ub2048/b2048   ub4096/b4096
#       d8192 count             0.77 s        2.48 s (3.2x)  5.18 s (6.7x)
#       d8192 copy              1.35 s        3.00 s (2.2x)  5.63 s (4.2x)
#       d36k  count             1.18 s        4.22 s (3.6x)  8.53 s (7.2x)
#       d36k  copy              1.98 s        4.94 s (2.5x)  9.25 s (4.7x)
#
#   **-ub 512 is 2.2-7.2x faster to a warm turn while losing the prefill-rate
#   contest by 25-45 %.** Decode agrees quietly: prose 37.3 -> 33.5 -> 33.2
#   at d36k. GTT rises 28.95 -> 30.75 -> 33.17 GiB.
#
#   WHY THE BROUGHT RECOMMENDATION SAYS OTHERWISE, and it is not a mistake on
#   their part: their figure comes from llama-bench, which has NO PROMPT CACHE
#   at all. It measures the rate and cannot see the reuse — which is exactly
#   what bench/suites/flag-ab.py's own docstring warns about when it calls its
#   winners SCREENING results that must survive the serving profile. This is
#   that survival test, and the screening result did not survive it.
#
#   The cache figures are also handicapped in -ub 512's DISfavour: its run
#   happened to start warmer (12.7 % against 0.0 % cached at d512), so the
#   gap above is a floor rather than a ceiling.
#
# -fa on, -fit off: carried from the house shape and NOT measured on this
#   model. -fit off is not a tuning at all but a refusal to let the fitter
#   guess (flashnext.env carries the crash report it comes from).
# -cram 32768: RAM-backed prompt cache, same as qwen38. Cheap here in a way
#   it is not for flashnext — the weights are 35.8 GiB rather than 103.7, so
#   the host side has room it has never had before. NOT measured; -cram is a
#   ceiling, and what it actually buys is a separate question
#   (setup/README.md, "What -cram buys").
# --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false}: thinking OFF by default,
#   with MODES above able to turn it on per request. Same arrangement as
#   qwen38 and flashnext.
#
# SPECULATION: THE MODEL'S OWN MTP HEAD, PLUS THE N-GRAM DRAFTER.
#
# The checkpoint has an MTP head (mtp_num_hidden_layers: 1) and unsloth throws
# it away on conversion (--no-nextn), so getting it meant a different file —
# see MODEL_SOURCE. It is inside the main GGUF, which is why there is no -md
# here: `--spec-type draft-mtp` finds it in the model it is already serving.
# The same shape qwen38 uses.
#
#   WHAT THE HEAD IS WORTH, measured 04.09.2026 as ONE VARIABLE — same file,
#   same binary, same window, same flags, `draft-mtp` added and nothing else.
#   bench/reports/2026-09-04_1152_speed_qwen36-mtpq4-rocm-mtp against
#   bench/reports/2026-09-04_1154_speed_qwen36-mtpq4-rocm-ngramonly. Decode, t/s:
#
#                     ngram only      + MTP head     draft accepted
#     prose  d512        36.0            44.9         --  -> 83.3 %
#     prose  d8192       33.0            37.8        5.5 % -> 45.2 %
#     prose  d36k        29.0            37.3        4.7 % -> 49.2 %
#     count  d512       201.2           265.0
#     count  d8192      192.5           261.8
#     count  d36k       162.1           212.7
#     copy   d512       285.0           171.7
#     copy   d8192      265.7           263.8
#     copy   d36k        95.9           215.1
#
#   THE ACCEPTANCE COLUMN IS THE FINDING, not the t/s. An n-gram drafter
#   drafts FROM THE PROMPT, so on novel text it has nothing to offer and its
#   acceptance is 4.7-5.5 %; the model's own head predicts and reaches 45-49 %
#   at the same depths. That is why `prose` — the one column speculation could
#   not previously touch, and the floor of everything this machine does —
#   gains 15-29 %. `count` gains 31-36 %. `copy` is a wash where its cells are
#   trustworthy (d8192, 265.7 against 263.8) and both of its other cells carry
#   speed.py's spread warning on the control side, so no factor should be read
#   off them.
#
#   THE HEAD COSTS about 1.9 GiB of GTT (27.71 against 25.84 for a plain Q4)
#   and about 4 % of prefill (589.2 against 623.5 at d36k) — it is a second
#   graph and it runs.
#
#   BOTH DRAFTERS ARE KEPT. `--spec-type` accumulates, so the list is
#   draft-mtp AND ngram-mod, which is what qwen38 has served since August:
#   they cover different shapes and the measurement above was taken with
#   ngram-mod present in both arms, so it says what the HEAD adds on top.
#   `--spec-draft-p-min 0.75` stays for the reason measured earlier in this
#   file — it stops a drafter running where it cannot win.
#
#   IT DID NOT HANG. That is not a formality: setup/defects.json carries
#   `flashnext-mtp-serving-shape-hangs`, where this exact kind of shape
#   produced zero tokens in 39 minutes at 97 % GPU. That entry is scoped to
#   qwen4exp and to the PRE-#27941 memory path, which is why LLAMA_BIN below
#   names a post-#27941 build and why the pre-#27941 house build was never
#   asked this question. Four full speed runs completed here without one.
#
LLAMA_ARGS=--alias qwen36 -m @MODELS@/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MTP-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf \
  --spec-type draft-mtp,ngram-mod --spec-draft-n-max 4 \
  --spec-ngram-mod-n-min 24 --spec-draft-p-min 0.75 \
  -ngl 999 -fa on -fit off -ub 512 -b 2048 -c 262144 -np 1 -cram 32768 \
  --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false} \
  --slot-save-path @HOME@/.cache/llama-slots \
  --jinja --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080
# CARRIED FROM flashnext.env, NOT MEASURED HERE. The MTP path on ROCm below
# 7.13 needs graphs off: with them on, the draft path rebuilt the HIP graph
# per token and measured 0.33 t/s on another tree. Every MTP figure in this
# file was taken with it set, so it is part of what was measured — but nobody
# has tried the other side on THIS model, and if somebody does, that is a
# flag-ab with one variable and not a guess.
GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1
# Backend: ROCm, and a PINNED build rather than the `build-rocm-patched`
# symlink. Both halves were measured 04.09.2026 and both need saying.
#
# WHY THE PIN, which CLAUDE.md permits only with a written retirement
# condition. The house build is b10702-11-gc799f1014 and it is PRE-#27941.
# setup/defects.json carries `flashnext-mtp-serving-shape-hangs`: a draft-mtp
# serving shape that produced zero tokens in 39 minutes at 97 % GPU, and the
# investigation put the pre-#27941 memory path in the condition. That entry
# is scoped to qwen4exp, so it does not PROVE anything about this model — it
# is a reason not to find out with a serving profile. Everything here was
# measured on the post-#27941 build below, which is the one flashnext has
# been serving MTP from since 02.09.
#   AND THE PIN TURNED OUT TO BE NECESSARY RATHER THAN CAUTIOUS. Measured
#   04.09.2026 by accident, while screening -ub through flag-ab, which runs
#   llama-bench out of build-rocm-patched: **b10702 cannot LOAD this file at
#   all** — six arms across two interleaved rounds, every one of them
#   `llama_bench: error: failed to load model`, in 0 s. The same file loads
#   and serves on the pinned build below, four full speed runs and one
#   depth-correctness run. The REASON was not captured: llama-bench prints
#   none beyond that line, and reproducing it live would mean allocating GTT
#   for a 21.7 GiB model beside flashnext's 91 — the shape that has taken
#   this machine down three times. So: an observation with a known cause
#   missing, not an explanation.
#   RETIREMENT CONDITION: this pin goes away when the house build is
#   post-#27941, LOADS this file, and one depth-correctness run on THIS
#   model's serving shape comes back green on it. That is one rebuild and one suite, and until
#   somebody spends it the pin is the cheaper half of the trade.
#   The pin is to a FOREIGN LINEAGE (drluoto plus this repo's two prompt-cache
#   commits) and inherits flashnext's own retirement condition with it: if
#   llama.cpp#28276 is fixed upstream, the lineage is rebuilt without those
#   two commits and this line follows.
#
# THE BACKEND ITSELF IS SPLIT, and four measurements say the same thing from
# two directions. bench/reports/2026-09-04_0942_sweep_qwen36 (Q4, 2x2 at
# -c 65536), …_1048/_1051 (Q8), …_1152/_1157 (MTP-Q4). Decode, t/s:
#
#                          prose            count            copy
#                      d512  8k  36k    d512  8k   36k    d512  8k   36k
#   plain Q4  rocm      38.1 35.3 30.8   47.6 35.2 31.6   36.7 34.3 30.7
#   plain Q4  vulkan    55.1 51.5 44.9   54.1 51.3 44.9   54.0 51.3 45.0
#   ngram Q4  rocm      39.0 39.4 36.0  223.3 212.2 178.2 105.3 285.6 233.0
#   ngram Q4  vulkan    57.0 48.8 41.4  213.0 203.4 172.6 108.9 226.8 112.1
#   MTP-Q4    rocm      44.9 37.8 37.3  265.0 261.8 212.7 171.7 263.8 215.1
#   MTP-Q4    vulkan    53.5 45.0 37.8  231.7 232.0 192.5 154.1 212.1 183.9
#
#   VULKAN OWNS THE FLOOR. With no drafter at all it wins by 1.45x, repeating
#   to two decimals at three depths with no spread warning on either cell —
#   twice this machine's ~22 % resolution floor. It keeps a smaller version of
#   that lead wherever acceptance is low: +19 % on prose at d512 and d8192
#   even with the MTP head, and a tie at d36k.
#
#   ROCM OWNS EVERYTHING SPECULATION REACHES, and that is the machine's actual
#   workload: count +10 % at every depth, copy +11 to +17 %. Prefill splits
#   too — ROCm at shallow depth, Vulkan at d36k (685.9 against 589.2).
#
#   SO ROCM SERVES, and this stays the weakest-held line in the file. TWO
#   THINGS HANDICAP THE VULKAN ARM AND BOTH UNDERSTATE IT:
#     * build-vulkan is commit 54ee5ee of 22.08. against the ROCm arm's
#       b10750-10 — on a stack where a build difference has been worth 25 %.
#     * the RADV APU heap option is NOT set on this machine: there is no
#       drirc anywhere (checked 04.09.2026; Mesa 26.1.7, so the option
#       exists). Strix Halo operators report it as required for Vulkan on a
#       unified-memory APU. Setting it changes GPU memory behaviour for every
#       Vulkan client including the desktop, on a machine that has frozen
#       three times over GTT — an operator decision, not a repo one.
#   What would settle it: rebuild Vulkan on the ROCm arm's commit, decide the
#   driconf option, and re-run through bench/suites/decode-rate.py — seven
#   rounds, not three, which is the instrument that exists because a
#   three-round mean once read a 12 % regression that was not there.
LLAMA_BIN=llama.cpp/build-rocm-cachelookup-b10750-10-g6bbb3eecf/bin/llama-server
#
# --- open points, in the order they block something -------------------------
#
#   [x] MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN, MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB, MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB —
#       done 04.09.2026, two-point, both points agreeing on the base.
#   [x] Is it actually FASTER than flashnext? Yes, and uniformly — in the
#       shape this profile now serves it is 1.9x to 2.9x on ALL TWELVE
#       measured cells, at 27.7 GiB of GTT against flashnext's 91.0:
#
#                       qwen36 MTP-Q4        flashnext        factor
#         prose      44.9  37.8  37.3    18.6  19.4  13.9    1.9-2.7x
#         count     265.0 261.8 212.7   112.3 104.8  85.1    2.4-2.5x
#         copy      171.7 263.8 215.1    60.7 103.4  86.6    2.5-2.8x
#         prefill   636.6 854.5 589.2   219.5 290.4 213.9    2.8-2.9x
#
#       There is no weak column left, which was not true before the MTP head:
#       with the n-gram drafter alone, prose sat at 29-35 t/s while copy was
#       already past 200. bench/reports/2026-09-04_0927_speed_flashnext-live-
#       04.09 is the incumbent's half, taken the same morning on the machine's
#       own production shape rather than read out of an older report.
#   [x] `--spec-type` — measured three ways (nospec, ngram-mod, +MTP head),
#       one variable at a time, see the SPECULATION block.
#   [x] AN MTP HEAD — found, measured, adopted. The sidecar route is closed
#       (defect `qwen36-sidecar-mtp-head-has-no-embeddings`); the head that
#       works lives inside havenoammo's full GGUF and is worth 15-29 % on
#       prose, 31-36 % on count, and a rise in draft acceptance there from
#       4.7 % to 49.2 %.
#   [x] A LARGER QUANT — measured (Q8 costs 5-8 %) and NOT taken, because the
#       operator's stated priority is throughput. One line reverses it and
#       the file is on disk.
#
#   [ ] IS THE QUALITY SUFFICIENT — THE ONLY QUESTION LEFT, AND THE ONE THIS
#       PROFILE CANNOT ANSWER. Nothing above is a quality statement. The
#       published figures point the other way: on agentic coding benchmarks
#       Qwen3.6-35B-A3B is BELOW the incumbent — SWE-bench Multilingual 67.2
#       against Flash-Next's 81.0, SWE-bench Pro 49.5 against 62.5 (vendor
#       and third-party numbers, read 04.09.2026, NOT measured here and not
#       comparable across sources without care). 3B active parameters against
#       6B is exactly why it is fast, and plausibly also why it is weaker.
#       This is an operator judgement on real work, not a benchmark this repo
#       can run — see the note at the top of bench/README.md for why the
#       model battery was removed rather than repaired.
#       AND SINCE 04.09. THERE IS A SECOND UNKNOWN STACKED ON THE FIRST: the
#       file this profile serves is a third-party quant with no published
#       imatrix (MODEL_SOURCE). It was trusted for its STRUCTURE, which was
#       read off it; its calibration is unverifiable. That makes
#       depth-correctness below more load-bearing than it would otherwise be.
#   [x] DEPTH-CORRECTNESS: RUN, AND GREEN — 24 of 24. Every anchor at every
#       depth to 99,390 tokens (first, middle, most-recent) plus the tool
#       definitions, on the exact serving shape.
#       bench/reports/2026-09-04_1211_depth-correctness_qwen36-mtpq4-rocm.
#       The upstream failure pattern this suite exists for — correct when
#       shallow, confabulated tool definitions past ~29k — does not appear.
#       WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT SETTLE: it rules out a BROKEN quant, not a
#       WORSE one. A file whose calibration is subtly off still finds a needle
#       it can see. So the provenance point above stands undiminished; this
#       removes the failure mode that would have been catastrophic and silent,
#       and leaves the one that is merely invisible.
#   [ ] The gateway's passive degeneracy check on real traffic — the other
#       half, and it needs the model to actually serve for a while.
#   [ ] CONVERT IT OURSELVES — the clean version of the quant this profile
#       serves. `conversion/qwen.py:636` registers this exact architecture and
#       its chain carries `supports_mtp_export = True`, so the local converter
#       can keep the head unsloth drops with `--no-nextn`, from the original
#       26-shard checkpoint. That buys known provenance; it costs unsloth's UD
#       recipe, which matters little at Q8 and a great deal at Q4.
#       `llama-quantize` and `llama-imatrix` exist in build-vulkan and the
#       older ROCm builds — the current pin does not ship them, so this needs
#       a rebuild with the target. NOT TRIED: neither the conversion nor the
#       serving.
#   [~] The BACKEND: measured 04.09.2026 and SPLIT — see the LLAMA_BIN block.
#       ROCm stays on the copy column; Vulkan wins the unspeculated floor by
#       1.45x while handicapped by an older build and a missing driver
#       option. Not closed, and the named follow-up is in that block.
#   [x] -ub 512 / -b 2048 — MEASURED ON THIS MODEL and kept, against the
#       brought recommendation of -b 4096 -ub 4096, which loses by 4.2-7.2x
#       on what a warm turn actually costs while winning the prefill RATE.
#       The full account is beside the flag. -cram 32768 is still carried and
#       unmeasured; -fa on and -fit off likewise.
#   [ ] q8_0 KV. Same handover reports +151 % at 64k. This repo's objection
#       to it is a CORRECTNESS one — q8 KV dropped the long-context task on
#       qwen38 (docs/HANDOVER.md, "Do not try again") — so the speed claim
#       cannot be acted on before that question is re-asked on this model.
#   [x] A LARGER QUANT — measured and adopted. UD-Q8_K_XL costs 5-8 %, not
#       half; the whole account is under MODEL_SOURCE. Note this is the one
#       decision in the file that a change of PRIORITY would reverse rather
#       than a change of evidence: if maximum throughput matters more than
#       fidelity, Q4 is 5-8 % faster and the file is still on disk.
#
#   AND ONE THING THAT MUST NOT BE COPIED FROM THAT HANDOVER: it launches
#   with `-np 3 --kv-unified`. Two slots on this GPU are a MEASURED defect
#   here, not a preference — the gfx1151 HIP race (llama.cpp #27579, root
#   cause #27572) turns every answer into '////', and slot restore with two
#   slots was corrupt 4/4 with a populated store. The matrix is in
#   setup/env/qwen38.env. -np 1 stays until somebody re-measures it.
#   [~] AN MTP HEAD — the sidecar route is CLOSED, the other one is open and
#       is the largest remaining throughput lever. havenoammo's sidecar
#       (35BA3B-MTP.gguf) cannot be a draft model: it is missing ten metadata
#       keys AND `token_embd.weight`, and only the metadata is repairable.
#       Registered as `qwen36-sidecar-mtp-head-has-no-embeddings`, with the
#       one-line check that answers it in seconds.
#       WHAT REMAINS: the same publisher's FULL models carry the MTP layer
#       inside the main GGUF (`Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MTP-UD-*.gguf`), used as -m
#       with `--spec-type draft-mtp` and no -md — the shape qwen38 serves.
#       Verified from that file's first 16 MB rather than by downloading it:
#       753 tensors, `nextn_predict_layers 1`, `block_count 41`, four
#       `blk.40.nextn.*`, `token_embd.weight` present.
#       AND THE CLEAN VERSION OF THE SAME THING: convert it ourselves.
#       `conversion/qwen.py:636` registers this exact architecture and its
#       chain carries `supports_mtp_export = True`, so the local converter
#       can keep the head that unsloth drops with `--no-nextn`. That buys
#       known provenance instead of a third-party quant with no imatrix;
#       it costs unsloth's UD recipe, which matters little at Q8 and a great
#       deal at Q4. Neither the conversion nor the serving has been tried.
#   [ ] VISION — mmproj blocked on the filename collision described under
#       MODEL_SOURCE until 04.09.2026; the fetcher now refuses rather than
#       destroys, so the remaining question is only which precision to fetch
#       under which name.
